@topic error.unhandled-error
title: UNHANDLED-ERROR: a call may fail and nothing handles or admits it
summary: A call that may complete with a declared error must handle it with `on NAME:`, admit it in the enclosing function's `errors` clause, or be proven unable to raise it; this one does none of those.
index: errors
alias: neither handled here nor admitted; may produce the declared error; unhandled declared error; error not handled
keywords: on, errors clause, handler, admit, propagate, fail, declared error, IndexNotFound, LowerUnderrun, UpperOverrun, Fn contract, top level, grouped
related: rule.unhandled-errors concept.errors proof.completions proof.list-bounds proof.slices proof.callable-contracts error.known-error error.undeclared-error concept.function-types rule.one-handler-per-error principle.errors-over-sentinels stdlib.std::list::get
docs: EXPLICIT-ERROR-COMPLETIONS.md STATIC-COMPLETION-PROOFS.md DIAGNOSTIC-RECOVERY.md VERIFIED-CALLABLE-CONTRACT-BOUNDARY.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC UNHANDLED-ERROR

== MEANING
Every call carries an obligation for each declared error it may complete
with. The call must do one of three things with each one:

  * handle it where the call is, with an `on NAME:` handler written
    under the call;
  * admit it: the enclosing function lists it in its `errors` clause
    (`fn f(x) -> int errors NAME:`), and the error passes on to f's
    callers, who carry the same obligation;
  * be proven unable to raise it: the completion analysis shows, from
    the facts known at this call, that the error cannot occur
    (proof.completions).

UNHANDLED-ERROR reports a call for which at least one possible error is
none of the three. The message names the whole unhandled set of the call,
sorted, in one diagnostic:

    this call may produce the declared error "Bad", which is neither
    handled here nor admitted by the enclosing function's own "errors"
    declaration
    this call may produce the declared errors "A", "B" and "C", which are
    neither handled here nor admitted ...

Handling one member of a group leaves exactly the rest. The same code is
reported, with its own wording, for `fail NAME` in a function that does not
declare NAME:

    "fail Bad" is not admitted here: the enclosing function does not
    declare "errors Bad"

The language's reason: a failure is a declared, typed completion, never an
exception that escapes unseen. A program says, at every call, what happens
when the call fails -- or the compiler has proven it cannot.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A call of your own fallible function from a function that neither
    handles nor declares its errors.
  * An intrinsic with declared errors whose precondition is not proven
    here. The usual ones:
        std::list::at, std::mutable_array::at, std::str::char_at   IndexNotFound
        std::str::substring                    LowerUnderrun, UpperOverrun
        argv()                            InvalidArgumentEncoding
        std::binary::parse                     LayoutLengthMismatch
    `xs.at(i)` is free of the obligation only in the forms proof.list-bounds
    lists, and std::str::substring only in those of proof.slices. A
    descending loop, an index written again in the guard and in the read,
    or one reduced by another container's size is not related to the
    length today:
        this call of "std::list::at" may produce the declared error
        "IndexNotFound", which is neither handled here nor admitted ...
  * A function body is checked once, for every argument: facts about the
    arguments of one call (`twice(4)`) are not carried into the callee's
    body, where the parameter is any int.
  * A call through a function value. A parameter typed with a structural
    function type is bounded by its contract,
    `Fn{args: [int], return: int, errors: [Bad]}`, so a call through it
    may produce exactly Bad (the message says "this call through a
    callable of function type ..."); a value the analysis cannot identify
    and no contract bounds is charged every error a callable passed
    through an untyped parameter may raise (proof.callable-contracts).
  * A call at the program's top level. The top level has no `errors`
    clause, so every possible error of a top-level call must be handled
    there or proven impossible.
  * A coroutine resume, or a rejected `static compile:` block, whose
    error (std::compiler::CompilerError) is not admitted where it stands.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
Read the error names in the message: they are canonical identities, so a
module's error appears qualified (`std::io::WriteFailed`). For an intrinsic,
`-help std::list::at` (or the symbol) shows its declared errors and the page
of the proof that removes them. For your own function, its `errors` clause
is the contract; the analysis may narrow it per call (an argument proven
non-negative removes an error raised only for negative input) but never
widens it.

== WHAT TO DO
Decide where the failure is meaningful:

  * Handle it where a sensible value exists: write the call, a colon and
    one `on NAME:` handler per error (one error per handler); the
    handler's value replaces the call's.
  * Admit it when the caller is the one who knows what to do: add the
    error to the enclosing function's `errors` clause.
  * Restate the code in a form the compiler proves (proof pages list the
    recognized forms), so that the error is impossible and no handler is
    needed. For list access, std::list::get(xs, i, default) is the handler
    written once.

Do not add a handler for an error the call can never complete normally
without: a call proven to always fail is KNOWN-ERROR, handler or not.

@example reject
error Negative

fn checked(x: int) -> int errors Negative:
    if x < 0:
        fail Negative
    x

fn twice(x: int) -> int:
    checked(x) * 2

twice(4)
@caption The caller neither handles nor admits Negative
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNHANDLED-ERROR
@at 9:5
@end

@example accept
error Negative

fn checked(x: int) -> int errors Negative:
    if x < 0:
        fail Negative
    x

fn or_zero(x: int) -> int:
    r = checked(x):
        on Negative:
            0
    r + 1

fn twice(x: int) -> int errors Negative:
    checked(x) * 2

[or_zero(-5), or_zero(4), checked(3)]
@caption Handled (or_zero), admitted (twice), proven away (checked(3))
@value [1, 5, 3]
@end

@example reject
error A
error B
error C

fn pick(x: int) -> int errors A, B, C:
    if x == 1:
        fail A
    if x == 2:
        fail B
    if x == 3:
        fail C
    x

fn use(x: int) -> int:
    pick(x)

0
@caption One diagnostic names the call's whole unhandled set
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNHANDLED-ERROR
@at 15:5
@message *the declared errors "A", "B" and "C", which are neither*
@end

@topic error.known-error
title: KNOWN-ERROR: a call proven to always fail, even under a handler
summary: A call that the compiler proves can never complete normally is rejected, whether or not a handler or an `errors` clause covers its error: a statically known failure is a mistake, not a branch.
index: errors
alias: can never complete normally; statically known failure; always fails; known failure
keywords: handler, on, KnownError, always, constant argument, literal, risky, negative test, static compile, std::test::rejects, parameter
related: rule.known-error concept.known-errors concept.negative-tests proof.completions error.unhandled-error concept.static-compile concept.testing stdlib.std::test::rejects proof.list-bounds
docs: STATIC-COMPLETION-PROOFS.md STATIC-COMPILE.md GOLDEN-TEST-FRAMEWORK.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC KNOWN-ERROR

== MEANING
The completion analysis computes, for each call, whether it may complete
normally under the facts proven for its arguments at that call. When it
cannot -- every path through the callee ends in a declared error -- the
call is KNOWN-ERROR:

    this call can never complete normally under the facts proven for its
    arguments here -- it always produces the declared error(s) Bad; a
    handler does not make a statically known failure legal

This is checked before handlers are even consulted. Botlish's completion
rule has three outcomes, and this is the third:

    provable success      no obligation: no handler is needed
    uncertain             the error is handled or admitted (UNHANDLED-ERROR)
    provable failure      a compile-time error: KNOWN-ERROR

Why a handler does not make it legal: a handler is how a program says what
happens when a call *may* fail at run time. For a call that always fails,
the call and its handler are an `if` whose condition the compiler has
already decided; the program would always run the handler, and the call
is dead weight that hides a mistake (a wrong constant, a stale argument).
An `errors` clause does not make it legal either: a function that calls
one that always fails always fails itself.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A literal argument that the callee rejects: `risky(-3)` where risky
    fails for negative input, `std::byte::from_int(999)`, `[1, 2].at(5)`.
  * A function whose only purpose is to fail (its whole body is `fail
    Bad`): every call of it is KNOWN-ERROR, even from a function that
    declares `errors Bad`. Write `fail Bad` where the failure is.
  * A test of an error path written as a literal call with a handler,
    such as `risky(-3): on Bad: -1` to check the failure path: the
    compiler rejects it (concept.negative-tests shows how to test it).

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message names the errors the call always produces. Look at the call's
arguments where it is written: constants, and bindings whose exact value
the compiler follows (a binding of a literal, arithmetic on literals). The
callee's guard (`if n < 0: fail Bad`) decides with those facts. Facts about
arguments are those of this call site only: inside a function, a parameter
is unknown, so the same call written there with the parameter is not known
to fail.

== WHAT TO DO
If the failure is a mistake, fix the argument. If you are writing a test of
the failure path (a negative test, concept.negative-tests), use one of the
two supported forms:

  * Route the failing input through a parameter: a small function that
    takes the input and handles the error. Inside it the call is uncertain,
    so the handler is legal; the test calls the function with the bad
    input.
  * Put the call in a `static compile:` block inside a function that
    declares `errors std::compiler::CompilerError`: the rejected block
    becomes that error, whose payload carries the code (KnownError), and the
    golden test framework checks it with std::test::rejects
    (concept.testing).

Inside a `static compile:` block, KNOWN-ERROR is the recoverable
diagnostic code std::compiler::DiagnosticCode::KnownError.

@example reject
error Bad

fn risky(n: int) -> int errors Bad:
    if n < 0:
        fail Bad
    n

risky(-3):
    on Bad:
        -1
@caption A handler does not make a known failure legal
@error CORE SEMANTIC KNOWN-ERROR
@at 8:1
@end

@example accept
error Bad

fn risky(n: int) -> int errors Bad:
    if n < 0:
        fail Bad
    n

fn attempt(n: int) -> int:
    risky(n):
        on Bad:
            -1

attempt(-3)
@caption The failing input routed through a parameter
@value -1
@end

@example accept
import std::compiler

error Bad

fn risky(n: int) -> int errors Bad:
    if n < 0:
        fail Bad
    n

fn probe() -> int errors std::compiler::CompilerError:
    static compile:
        risky(-3)

probe():
    on std::compiler::CompilerError:
        -1
@caption The rejection itself observed through static compile
@value -1
@end

@topic error.undeclared-error
title: UNDECLARED-ERROR: an error name that denotes no declared error
summary: An error named in `fail`, `on`, an `errors` clause or a function type is not a visible declared error, or an `error` declaration repeats an existing name.
index: errors
alias: unknown error; no error declaration is visible; error is already declared
keywords: error declaration, fail, on, errors clause, qualified, module error, builtin error, redeclare
related: concept.errors error.unhandled-error error.missing-error-payload concept.modules concept.imports error.duplicate error.missing-import
docs: EXPLICIT-ERROR-COMPLETIONS.md ERROR-PAYLOADS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC UNDECLARED-ERROR

== MEANING
An error is a nominal identity introduced by `error NAME` (with an
optional payload). Every place that names one -- `fail NAME`, a handler
`on NAME:`, an `errors` clause, a `Fn{..., errors: [...]}` type -- is
resolved once, in the namespace of the code that writes it, to a declared
error. When the spelling denotes none:

    unknown error "Nope": no "error Nope" declaration is visible

A module's error is named qualified outside that module
(`std::io::WriteFailed`, `std::compiler::CompilerError`); when the short
name matches a module's error, the message says so:

    unknown error "CompilerError": no "error CompilerError" declaration is
    visible here (a module's error is named qualified outside its module:
    std::compiler::CompilerError)

The same code reports a declaration that repeats an identity, including a
builtin error of the runtime (InvalidArgumentEncoding, IndexNotFound,
LowerUnderrun, UpperOverrun, LayoutLengthMismatch):

    error "Bad" is already declared
    error "IndexNotFound" is already declared (a builtin error of the
    runtime)

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A typo, or a `fail` written before its `error` declaration exists.
  * A module's error spelled by its short name outside the module. (A
    qualified name of a module that is not imported, such as
    `std::compiler::CompilerError` without `import std::compiler`, is
    MISSING-IMPORT instead.)
  * Declaring an error whose name is a builtin error.

== WHAT TO DO
Declare the error (`error Nope`, top level), spell a module's error with
its qualifier, import the module, or rename a declaration that collides.
There is no wildcard handler and no catch-all error.

@example reject
fn lookup(key: str) -> int:
    fail Missing

0
@caption No `error Missing` declaration exists
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNDECLARED-ERROR
@at 2:5
@end

@topic error.missing-error-payload
title: MISSING-ERROR-PAYLOAD: fail of an error with fields, without its payload
summary: An error declared with fields carries a payload, and `fail NAME` must construct it: `fail NAME {field: value, ...}`.
index: errors
alias: carries a payload; fail without payload
keywords: error payload, fields, fail, construct, struct literal
related: concept.error-payloads error.unexpected-error-payload concept.errors error.undeclared-error
docs: ERROR-PAYLOADS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC MISSING-ERROR-PAYLOAD

== MEANING
`error NotFound:` followed by `key: str` declares an error whose every
occurrence carries a value of those fields. The name alone is not that
value, so `fail NotFound` is rejected:

    error "NotFound" carries a payload, which "fail NotFound" must
    construct: write "fail NotFound {key: ...}" (the error's name alone is
    not its payload)

The payload is checked like a struct construction: every declared field
exactly once (MISSING-FIELD, UNKNOWN-FIELD, TYPE otherwise).

== WHAT TO DO
Construct the payload at the `fail`: `fail NotFound {key: key}`. If no
`fail` site has anything to say, the error needs no fields: remove them
from the declaration (and from handlers that bind them).

@example reject
error NotFound:
    key: str

fn lookup(key: str) -> int errors NotFound:
    fail NotFound

0
@caption The error declares a field; fail must supply it
@error CORE SEMANTIC MISSING-ERROR-PAYLOAD
@at 5:5
@end

@example accept
error NotFound:
    key: str

fn lookup(key: str) -> int errors NotFound:
    if key == "one":
        return 1
    fail NotFound {key: key}

fn missing_key(key: str) -> str:
    lookup(key):
        on NotFound {key: missing}:
            return missing
    "found"

[missing_key("two"), missing_key("one")]
@caption The payload constructed at fail, destructured in the handler
@value ["two", "found"]
@end

@topic error.unexpected-error-payload
title: UNEXPECTED-ERROR-PAYLOAD: a payload for an error that declares none
summary: An error declared without fields has no payload: `fail NAME {...}` cannot construct one and `on NAME details:` or `on NAME {a}:` has nothing to bind.
index: errors
alias: declares no payload; nothing to bind
keywords: error payload, fail, handler, details, destructure, fields
related: concept.error-payloads error.missing-error-payload concept.errors
docs: ERROR-PAYLOADS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC UNEXPECTED-ERROR-PAYLOAD

== MEANING
A payload-free error (`error Empty`, no field lines) is one identity and
nothing else. Two places can try to give it a value:

    fail Empty {reason: "none"}
        error "Empty" declares no payload: write "fail Empty" (to give it
        one, declare its fields: "error Empty:" followed by "field: Type"
        lines)
    on Empty details:          (or on Empty {reason}:)
        error "Empty" declares no payload: there is nothing to bind
        (write "on Empty:")

== WHAT TO DO
Write `fail Empty` and `on Empty:`; or, if the information is real, declare
the error's fields and construct them at every `fail`.

@example reject
error Empty

fn first(n: int) -> int errors Empty:
    if n == 0:
        fail Empty {reason: "none"}
    n

0
@caption Empty declares no fields
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNEXPECTED-ERROR-PAYLOAD
@at 5:9
@end

@topic error.break-outside-loop
title: BREAK-OUTSIDE-LOOP: break is not lexically inside a loop
summary: `break` must be written inside a loop body of the same function; a function boundary (a nested `fn`) between the break and the loop is not crossed.
index: errors
alias: break outside lexical loop; break outside loop
keywords: break, loop, nested function, closure, placement
related: rule.control-placement concept.loops error.continue-outside-loop error.return-outside-callable
docs: COLLECTING-LOOPS.md PAYLOAD-FREE-BREAK.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC BREAK-OUTSIDE-LOOP

== MEANING
`break` ends the innermost enclosing loop (a bare `loop:`, a collecting
loop, a lockstep loop). It is resolved lexically: the loop must enclose
the `break` in the same function. `if` branches and error handlers inside
the loop body do not count as a boundary; a nested `fn` does, because its
body runs whenever it is called, possibly after the loop has finished.

    break outside lexical loop

Core IR text reports the same code, at load time or when the call
boundary is crossed at run time.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * `break` in a function body or at the top level, with no loop.
  * `break` inside a helper function defined in a loop, meant to end the
    outer loop.

== WHAT TO DO
Move the `break` into the loop body. To end a loop from a helper, have the
helper return a value (a Bool, or an enum case) and `break` in the loop on
it. `break` never takes a value: a collecting loop's value is its List,
and a bare `break` ends it with the elements collected so far (`break 5`
is a SURFACE SYNTAX error).

@example reject
fn stop():
    break

0
@caption break in a function body with no loop
@error CORE SEMANTIC BREAK-OUTSIDE-LOOP
@at 2:5
@end

@topic error.continue-outside-loop
title: CONTINUE-OUTSIDE-LOOP: continue is not lexically inside a loop
summary: `continue` must be written inside a loop body of the same function; it cannot reach a loop across a nested function.
index: errors
alias: continue outside lexical loop; continue outside loop
keywords: continue, loop, nested function, placement
related: rule.control-placement concept.loops error.break-outside-loop error.return-outside-callable
docs: COLLECTING-LOOPS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC CONTINUE-OUTSIDE-LOOP

== MEANING
`continue` starts the next iteration of the innermost enclosing loop (in a
collecting loop the skipped iteration contributes nothing; in a lockstep
loop every clause advances). Like `break`, it is resolved lexically, within
one function:

    continue outside lexical loop

== WHAT TO DO
Move it into the loop body. A helper function cannot continue its caller's
loop: return a value and decide in the loop (`if skip?(x): continue`).

@example reject
fn skip():
    continue

0
@caption continue in a function body with no loop
@error CORE SEMANTIC CONTINUE-OUTSIDE-LOOP
@at 2:5
@end

@topic error.reorder-not-proven
title: REORDER-NOT-PROVEN: an order not known to hold the collection's members
summary: m.reorder(order) compiles only where the compiler knows ORDER holds exactly m's current keys (a set's elements): a MutableSet of the right type, or of the same length, is not enough.
index: errors
alias: order is not known to hold exactly; unproven order; reorder not proven
keywords: reorder, order, validate_order, OrderMismatch, MutableMap, MutableSet, permutation, members, keys, relation, proof
related: concept.collection-order proof.same-members concept.mutable-map concept.mutable-set stdlib.std::mutable_map::reorder stdlib.std::mutable_set::reorder
docs: PROVEN-COLLECTION-REORDERING.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC REORDER-NOT-PROVEN

== MEANING
    `c` is not known to hold exactly the current keys of `m`, which
    std::mutable_map::reorder requires of its order ...

A reorder trusts its order and checks nothing at run time, so the
compiler admits it only where it knows a relation between the two
particular values: the order's members are exactly the collection's
members, now. Its type says only which values may be members. The
relation comes from `m.order()`, from `m.validate_order(candidate)`'s
result or from a copy of either; a change to the collection's members
(set, remove, clear, add, a place call over it, for a context member a
call that may change the installed context) or to the order's, anywhere
on a path to the call, loses it, and so does a function boundary.

== WHAT TO DO
Take the order from `m.order()` and rearrange it, or build a candidate
and check it once with `m.validate_order(candidate)` -- handling (or
declaring) its error OrderMismatch -- and reorder by the result, with no
change to either value's members in between.

@example reject
import std::mutable_map
import std::mutable_set

m = std::mutable_map::create[str, int]()
m.set("pear", 1)
m.set("fig", 2)
c = std::mutable_set::create[str]()
c.add("fig")
c.add("pear")
m.reorder(c)
@caption The candidate holds the right keys, but nothing proved it
@error CORE SEMANTIC REORDER-NOT-PROVEN
@at 10:1
@end

@example accept
import std::mutable_map
import std::mutable_set

m = std::mutable_map::create[str, int]()
m.set("pear", 1)
m.set("fig", 2)
c = std::mutable_set::create[str]()
c.add("fig")
c.add("pear")
o = m.validate_order(c):
    on std::mutable_map::OrderMismatch:
        m.order()
m.reorder(o)
loop e in m:
    e.key
@caption Checked once, then trusted
@value ["fig", "pear"]
@end

@example reject
import std::mutable_map

m = std::mutable_map::create[str, int]()
o = m.order()
m.set("kiwi", 3)
m.reorder(o)
@caption A new key since the order was taken
@error CORE SEMANTIC REORDER-NOT-PROVEN
@at 6:1
@end

@topic error.return-outside-callable
title: RETURN-OUTSIDE-CALLABLE: return outside a function
summary: `return` leaves the enclosing function, so it is legal only inside a function body; the program's top level is not a function.
index: errors
alias: return outside callable invocation; return at top level
keywords: return, top level, function, program value, placement
related: rule.control-placement concept.functions concept.program-value error.break-outside-loop error.continue-outside-loop
docs: EXPLICIT-ERROR-COMPLETIONS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC RETURN-OUTSIDE-CALLABLE

== MEANING
`return EXPR` (or a bare `return`) completes the innermost enclosing
function with a value. Written at the top level of a program -- including
in a top-level `if` or loop -- it has no function to leave:

    return outside callable invocation

== WHAT TO DO
A program's value is its last expression (concept.program-value): write
the value as the last line instead of `return`. To leave early, put the
code in a function and call it.

@example reject
return 3
@caption The top level is not a function
@error CORE SEMANTIC RETURN-OUTSIDE-CALLABLE
@at 1:1
@end

@topic error.listloop-break-value
title: LISTLOOP-BREAK-VALUE: break with a value in a collecting loop (internal)
summary: An internal guard: HIR in which a `break` carries a value out of a collecting loop is rejected; Botlish source cannot produce it, because the parser rejects every `break` with a value.
index: errors
alias: break with a value is not valid inside a collecting loop
keywords: break, collecting loop, value, HIR, internal
related: error.break-outside-loop concept.loops error.surface-syntax
docs: RETURNING-ITERABLE-LOOPS.md PAYLOAD-FREE-BREAK.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC LISTLOOP-BREAK-VALUE

== MEANING
A collecting loop (`loop x in xs:`, `loop i from a to b:`, a lockstep loop)
has exactly one result type, the List of its body's values; a bare `break`
ends it with the List collected so far. A `break VALUE` would let one exit
replace that List with a value of an unrelated type, so the resolver
rejects it:

    break with a value is not valid inside a collecting loop (loop x in
    ..., loop i from ...): use a bare break to end the loop with the
    collected prefix

No Botlish source program reaches this check: the surface parser rejects
`break` followed by any value, in every kind of loop, as SURFACE SYNTAX
("\"break\" does not take a value"). The check defends HIR built by other
means than the source front end. If you see it from a source program,
report it as a compiler bug.

== WHAT TO DO
In source: write a bare `break`. To produce a different result when a loop
stops early, compute it after the loop (or in a function that `return`s
it).

@topic error.missing-context
title: MISSING-CONTEXT: a call needs a context that is not installed
summary: The called function (or one it calls) declares a context parameter, and no `with context` installation of that context precedes this call at the program's top level.
index: errors
alias: is required by this call but is not installed here; no installed context implements it; context not installed
keywords: with context, context parameter, context struct, context trait, std::io::IO, installation, chain, requirement, order
related: concept.contexts concept.context-traits proof.contexts rule.context-installation rule.sfiae error.not-a-context error.context-function-value error.context-installation-unsupported stdlib.module.std::io
docs: CONTEXTS.md CONTEXT-TRAITS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC MISSING-CONTEXT

== MEANING
A function that uses a value of the execution environment declares it in
its context section, `fn now(context clock: Clock)`, and every function
that calls it, directly or through others, requires that context too,
without writing anything. A call at the top level must find every context
its callee requires installed by an earlier `with context EXPR` line:

    Clock is required by this call but is not installed here (install it
    with "with context EXPR" before the call):
        report
         -> now
            directly requires Clock

The chain shows why: the shortest call path from the called function to
one that declares the context. For a context trait (std::io::IO, ...) the
message says that no installed context implements it, and lists the
installed contexts:

    std::io::IO is required by this call, but no installed context implements
    it (install one with "with context EXPR" before the call): ...

A trait operation's implementation counts like any callee: a call of a
trait-polymorphic function with a concrete witness requires what the
implementation its operation binds for that witness requires (another
witness's implementation is no part of it). The chain then names the
specialization and the step through the operation:

    Clock is required by this call but is not installed here (...):
        twice (for A)
         -> show, the implementation of Thing's operation show for A,
            called at f.bot:17:5
         -> now
            directly requires Clock

The reason for the check: a context is never looked up at run time. A
program that compiles has every context it uses installed before the code
that uses it runs.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A trait implementation calls a function that needs a context, and the
    program calls a trait-polymorphic function with that witness: the
    implementation's requirement is the call's. Installing the context is
    the fix; the operation never switches to another implementation because
    a context is missing.
  * The `with context` line is missing, or it comes after the call:
    installation takes effect from its line onward, never earlier.
  * A top-level binding (`start = now()`) evaluated before the
    installation.
  * A module's top-level code (a module binding's initializer) calls a
    context consumer: module code runs before the entry program installs
    anything.
  * Printing with std::io::print_line, which requires std::io::IO: install
    an implementation, e.g. `with context std::linux::io::create()` (native
    backends on Linux).

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
Follow the chain to the function that "directly requires" the context. The
`-contexts FILE.bot` option of botlish prints, for every function, the
contexts it uses and requires, and why.

== WHAT TO DO
Install the context at the entry program's top level, as a statement,
before the first call that needs it: `with context Clock {ticks: 0}`. Do
not pass the context as an argument: the context section is not part of
the call (`now()`, never `now(clock)`).

@example reject
context struct Clock:
    ticks: int

fn now(context clock: Clock) -> int:
    clock.ticks

fn report() -> int:
    now()

report()
@caption report requires Clock through now; nothing installs it
@error CORE SEMANTIC MISSING-CONTEXT
@at 10:1
@end

@example reject
context struct Clock:
    ticks: int

fn now(context clock: Clock) -> int:
    clock.ticks

trait Thing:
    fn show(t: Thing) -> int

struct A:
    v: int

fn show(t: A) -> int:
    t.v + now()

fn twice(t: Thing) -> int:
    t.show() + t.show()

twice(A {v: 1})
@caption twice's specialization for A requires Clock through A's show; nothing installs it
@error CORE SEMANTIC MISSING-CONTEXT
@at 19:1
@end

@example accept
context struct Clock:
    ticks: int

fn now(context clock: Clock) -> int:
    clock.ticks

fn report() -> int:
    now() + 1

with context Clock {ticks: 41}
report()
@caption Installed before the call
@value 42
@end

@topic error.not-a-context
title: NOT-A-CONTEXT: a context parameter or installation of a non-context type
summary: Only a value of a type declared `context struct` can be installed with `with context` or received in a context section (or a `context trait` there); this type is neither.
index: errors
alias: is not a context; only a value of a type declared context struct
keywords: context struct, context parameter, with context, struct, trait, context trait
related: concept.contexts concept.context-traits error.missing-context error.context-type-not-exact error.duplicate-context
docs: CONTEXTS.md CONTEXT-TRAITS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC NOT-A-CONTEXT

== MEANING
`context` is a struct modifier: `context struct Clock:` declares a type
whose values may be installed into the execution environment. A context
section entry (`context clock: Clock`) and a `with context EXPR` statement
accept only such a type (and, in a context section, a context trait):

    context parameter "clock": Clock is not a context (only a value of a
    type declared "context struct" can be a context)
    with context: a value of type int is not a context (only a value of a
    type declared "context struct" can be installed)

A value trait (an ordinary `trait`) in a context section is reported
under this code as well: it abstracts over values, not over the
environment.

== WHAT TO DO
Declare the struct with the modifier (`context struct Clock:`), or, if the
value is an ordinary input, make it an ordinary parameter and pass it.

@example reject
struct Clock:
    ticks: int

fn now(context clock: Clock) -> int:
    clock.ticks

0
@caption Clock is an ordinary struct
@error CORE SEMANTIC NOT-A-CONTEXT
@at 4:23
@end

@topic error.duplicate-context
title: DUPLICATE-CONTEXT: a context type installed twice
summary: A context is identified by its type, and at most one value of a type is installed: a second `with context` of the same type is rejected, never a replacement.
index: errors
alias: is already installed in this scope; context installed twice
keywords: with context, installation, replace, shadow, identity
related: concept.contexts rule.context-installation error.missing-context error.not-a-context
docs: CONTEXTS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC DUPLICATE-CONTEXT

== MEANING
There is at most one installed value of each context type: no named
instances, no replacement, no shadowing, no last-one-wins.

    Clock is already installed in this scope

The installation is static: the compiler knows which value every
requirement reads, and a second installation would make that depend on
position. (A program run without static checking gets the same failure
at run time from the Tcl backends.)

== WHAT TO DO
Install each context type once. To give part of the program a different
value, pass it as an ordinary argument, or declare a second context struct
type for the second role.

@example reject
context struct Clock:
    ticks: int

with context Clock {ticks: 1}
with context Clock {ticks: 2}
0
@caption The second installation of Clock
@error CORE SEMANTIC DUPLICATE-CONTEXT
@at 5:1
@end

@topic error.context-type-not-exact
title: CONTEXT-TYPE-NOT-EXACT: the installed value has no one exact context type
summary: `with context EXPR` installs by the value's static type, which must be exactly one named context struct, not an anonymous struct, `any` or a choice of types.
index: errors
alias: must have one exact context-struct type
keywords: with context, anonymous struct, any, union, inference, type annotation
related: concept.contexts error.not-a-context error.duplicate-context concept.types
docs: CONTEXTS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC CONTEXT-TYPE-NOT-EXACT

== MEANING
The installed type is inferred from the expression, and it is the
context's identity. When inference gives an anonymous struct, `any`, or a
type that may be one of several structs, there is no single identity to
install under:

    with context: the installed value must have one exact context-struct
    type, but its type is struct{ticks: int}

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * An anonymous struct literal `{ticks: 1}` instead of `Clock {ticks: 1}`.
  * A constructor function without a result annotation whose branches
    build different structs, or whose result the compiler infers as any.

== WHAT TO DO
Construct the named context struct, or give the constructor a declared
result type (`fn make() -> Clock:`).

@example reject
context struct Clock:
    ticks: int

with context {ticks: 1}
0
@caption An anonymous struct is not Clock
@error CORE SEMANTIC CONTEXT-TYPE-NOT-EXACT
@at 4:1
@end

@topic error.context-binding-collision
title: CONTEXT-BINDING-COLLISION: a context parameter named like another parameter
summary: Context parameters are ordinary locals of the function and share its one namespace, so one may not have the name of an ordinary parameter, a flag or another context parameter.
index: errors
alias: context parameter has the name of
keywords: context parameter, parameter, flag, name, namespace, local
related: concept.contexts error.duplicate-context-parameter error.duplicate concept.flags
docs: CONTEXTS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC CONTEXT-BINDING-COLLISION

== MEANING
Inside the function a context parameter is an ordinary immutable local.
Its name must therefore be distinct from every other parameter of the
function:

    context parameter "n" has the name of a parameter or flag of the same
    function: context parameters are ordinary locals of the function and
    share its one namespace

== WHAT TO DO
Rename the context parameter. Its name is local only: the context's
identity is its type, so renaming it changes nothing for callers.

@example reject
context struct Clock:
    ticks: int

fn now(n, context n: Clock) -> int:
    n.ticks

0
@caption n is already the ordinary parameter
@error CORE SEMANTIC CONTEXT-BINDING-COLLISION
@at 4:19
@end

@topic error.duplicate-context-parameter
title: DUPLICATE-CONTEXT-PARAMETER: one context type declared twice in a function
summary: A function receives at most one value of each context type, so its context section may name a type (or a context trait) only once, whatever the local names.
index: errors
alias: context is declared twice
keywords: context section, context parameter, context trait, identity, type
related: concept.contexts error.context-binding-collision error.malformed-context-section
docs: CONTEXTS.md CONTEXT-TRAITS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC DUPLICATE-CONTEXT-PARAMETER

== MEANING
A context is identified by its type and installed once, so two entries of
one type would be two names for the same value:

    context Clock is declared twice (as "a" and "b"): a function receives
    at most one value of each context type, whatever the local names

== WHAT TO DO
Keep one entry; bind a second local name in the body if one is wanted
(`b = a`). Two genuinely different values need two context types.

@example reject
context struct Clock:
    ticks: int

fn now(context a: Clock, b: Clock) -> int:
    a.ticks

0
@caption Clock twice in one context section
@error CORE SEMANTIC DUPLICATE-CONTEXT-PARAMETER
@at 4:29
@end

@topic error.context-function-value
title: CONTEXT-FUNCTION-VALUE: a context-requiring function used as a value
summary: A function that requires a context may only be called (or aliased by a statement), never passed, returned or stored: a call through a function value could not prove the context installed.
index: errors
alias: requires context so it can only be called
keywords: function value, higher-order, callback, pass, return, store, closure, context requirement
related: concept.contexts concept.closures concept.function-types error.missing-context error.direct-callable-value
docs: CONTEXTS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC CONTEXT-FUNCTION-VALUE

== MEANING
Context requirements are computed over exact call edges: a call of a
function, an alias of one (`g = f`), a module function. A call through a
parameter, a field or a list element could reach any function, and context
requirements are not part of function types, so the analysis could not
see what it needs. The frontier is closed at the source:

    function now requires context Clock, so it can only be called (or
    bound to another name as a statement): it cannot be passed, returned
    or stored as a value, because a call through an unknown function value
    could not prove that context installed

This includes returning the function as a body's or the program's last
expression, choosing it in an `if` value, and closures that call a
context consumer (they require the context too). A function nested in a
trait-polymorphic function is judged in every specialization: one whose
trait operation binds, for some witness, an implementation that needs a
context is reported for that specialization, `function inner (for A)
requires context ...`.

== WHAT TO DO
Pass the data instead of the function, or wrap the work so the
higher-order code only receives context-free functions: call the consumer
first and pass its result, or restructure so the consumer is called
directly where the context is known.

@example reject
context struct Clock:
    ticks: int

fn now(context clock: Clock) -> int:
    clock.ticks

fn twice(f) -> int:
    f() + f()

with context Clock {ticks: 1}
twice(now)
@caption now passed as an argument
@error CORE SEMANTIC CONTEXT-FUNCTION-VALUE
@at 11:7
@end

@topic error.context-installation-unsupported
title: CONTEXT-INSTALLATION-UNSUPPORTED: with context outside the top level
summary: `with context EXPR` is supported only as a statement at the entry program's top level: not in a function, a loop, an `if` branch, a handler, an expression or a module.
index: errors
alias: context installation is currently supported only; with context inside a function
keywords: with context, installation, top level, function, loop, branch, module
related: rule.context-installation concept.contexts error.missing-context error.duplicate-context concept.modules
docs: CONTEXTS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC CONTEXT-INSTALLATION-UNSUPPORTED
code: SURFACE MODULE CONTEXT-INSTALLATION-UNSUPPORTED

== MEANING
Installation is deliberately narrow in this version of the language: the
application decides its execution environment, once, at the top of the
entry program. Anywhere else is rejected with where it was found:

    context installation is currently supported only in the entry
    program's top-level scope, as a statement (this one is inside a
    function)

The other places named are "inside a loop", "inside an if branch or error
handler" and "inside an expression". In an imported module the loader
reports it before anything is compiled, as SURFACE MODULE
CONTEXT-INSTALLATION-UNSUPPORTED ("this one is inside a module: NAME,
PATH"): a library never installs contexts for the program that imports
it.

== WHAT TO DO
Move the `with context` line to the entry program's top level, before the
calls that need it. A library offers a constructor
(`std::linux::io::create()`) and the program installs its result.

@example reject
context struct Clock:
    ticks: int

fn setup():
    with context Clock {ticks: 1}
    0

0
@caption Installation inside a function
@error CORE SEMANTIC CONTEXT-INSTALLATION-UNSUPPORTED
@at 5:5
@end

@topic error.module-context
title: MODULE-CONTEXT: a module binding's initializer is not context-free
summary: A module's top-level value bindings are computed once, before the entry program runs, so their initializers may only use operations classified context-free.
index: errors
alias: module binding initializer must be context-free
keywords: module binding, module, initializer, context-free, native, top-level value
related: rule.module-bindings concept.modules error.module-immutable error.missing-context
docs: MODULE-BINDINGS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC MODULE-CONTEXT

== MEANING
A module may bind values at its top level (`table = [1, 2, 3]`). The
compiler proves two facts about each: its initializer is context-free, and
the retained value is immutable (MODULE-IMMUTABLE). Context-free means
that every operation the initializer runs -- directly or through the
functions it calls -- is a literal, a reference, control flow, a call of
a function whose body is context-free, or an intrinsic registered as
context-free. Anything else (an unclassified intrinsic, a call through a
function value, a context load) is rejected, with the first offending
operation named:

    module binding initializer must be context-free: native operation
    "context#load" is not classified context-free
    module binding initializer must be context-free: call target is not
    statically known

The check applies to imported modules only (the entry program's top
level is not a module), so it cannot be shown by a single-file example.
In a module m2.bot,

    context struct Clock:
        ticks: int
    fn now(context clock: Clock) -> int:
        clock.ticks
    start = now()

reports MISSING-CONTEXT at the call and MODULE-CONTEXT at the binding.

== WHAT TO DO
Make the module value a function (`fn start() -> int:` with the body
`now()`), which runs when it is called, under the program's installed
contexts; or compute the value from literals and context-free operations.

@topic error.malformed-context-section
title: MALFORMED-CONTEXT-SECTION: a parameter list's context section is malformed
summary: The context section comes last in a parameter list, once, as `context NAME: Type, NAME: Type`; a flag after it, a second section or an entry without its type is a syntax error.
index: errors
alias: context section cannot follow; needs its context type
keywords: context section, parameter list, flags, order, type, syntax
related: concept.contexts concept.flags error.malformed-flag-section error.duplicate-context-parameter error.surface-syntax
docs: CONTEXTS.md FLAGS.md
code: MALFORMED-CONTEXT-SECTION
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | MALFORMED-CONTEXT-SECTION: *

== MEANING
A parameter list is a sequence of sections in one order: ordinary
parameters, then flags, then the context section. The context section
opens with the word `context` and holds `NAME: Type` entries; the type is
required, because it is the context's identity. The parser reports, at the
offender (printed as SURFACE SYNTAX, the message starting with the code):

    a "flags" section cannot follow the context section: a parameter list
    is ordinary parameters, then flags, then context
    a "context" section cannot follow the context section: ... (list
    several contexts in one section: "context a: A, b: B")
    context parameter "clock" needs its context type: write "clock: Type"
    (a context is identified by its type)

== WHAT TO DO
Write `fn f(x, flags :quick, context a: A, b: B)`: one context section,
last, every entry typed.

@example reject
context struct Clock:
    ticks: int

fn now(context clock: Clock, flags :fast) -> int:
    clock.ticks

0
@caption A flag section after the context section
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 4:30
@end

@topic error.malformed-variadic-section
title: MALFORMED-VARIADIC-SECTION: a parameter list's variadic section is malformed
summary: The variadic section `variadic NAME: List[T]` is the last parameter binding, at most once; anything after it but a resume clause, or an untyped entry, is a syntax error.
index: errors
alias: variadic parameter must be the last; at most one variadic parameter; needs its List type
keywords: variadic, parameter list, section order, List, trait requirement, syntax
related: concept.variadics concept.flags error.malformed-flag-section error.malformed-context-section error.surface-syntax
docs: VARIADICS-VERTICAL-SLICE-1.md FLAGS.md
code: MALFORMED-VARIADIC-SECTION
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | MALFORMED-VARIADIC-SECTION: *

== MEANING
A parameter list is a sequence of sections in one order: ordinary
parameters, flags, the context section, then the variadic parameter, which
only a resume clause may follow. The section opens with the word
`variadic` followed by a name, and its one entry is `NAME: List[T]` or
`NAME: List`. The parser reports, at the offender (printed as SURFACE
SYNTAX, the message starting with the code):

    the variadic parameter "xs" must be the last parameter of the list,
    found ... after it (a parameter list is ordinary parameters, then
    flags, then context, then the variadic parameter)
    a function declares at most one variadic parameter
    variadic parameter "xs" needs its List type: write "variadic xs:
    List[T]" (or "List" for an element type each call imprints)
    requirement "f" of trait "T" declares a variadic parameter: a trait
    requirement is a fixed signature

`variadic` alone (`fn f(variadic)`) or with a colon (`fn f(variadic: int)`)
is an ordinary parameter named variadic, as everywhere else.

== WHAT TO DO
Write `fn f(a, flags :q, context io: Log, variadic xs: List[int])`: one
variadic section, last, its entry typed as a List. A declared type that is
no List (`variadic xs: int`) is a TYPE error of the resolver instead.

@example reject
fn f(variadic xs: List[int], y: int) -> int:
    y

0
@caption An ordinary parameter after the variadic section
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 1:30
@end

@topic error.direct
title: DIRECT: a module uses direct authority without acknowledging it
summary: A call into a direct module (linux, a raw syscall transport, a retaining C function) is made by a function that requires no context of its own module, and the module is not declared `direct module`.
index: errors
alias: uses direct authority; undischarged direct edge
keywords: direct module, linux, syscall, authority, context, mediate, contextualize, capability, extern c fn, captures
related: concept.direct-modules concept.contexts error.stale-direct-module error.direct-callable-value error.direct-module-position concept.c-ffi stdlib.module.std::linux
docs: DIRECT-MODULES.md CONTEXTS.md C-FFI.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC DIRECT

== MEANING
Raw, ambient authority -- a syscall, a raw descriptor, a C call that may
keep a pointer -- is acknowledged at the module boundary. A module is
designated direct by `direct module` as its file's first line (or, for
the raw syscall transports, by the compiler). A call in module U of a
callable of another direct module is a direct edge of U; it is mediated
when the function making the call requires a context (a context struct or
context trait) that U declares itself. An unmediated edge makes U
inferred direct, and then its declaration must say so:

    the entry program uses direct authority: function "emit" calls
    std::linux::write, a callable of the direct module "std::linux", without a
    context defined by the entry program; require one of this module's
    own contexts ("context NAME: T", T a context struct or context trait
    declared by the entry program) in "emit", the function that makes the
    direct call, or declare the module direct with `direct module` as its
    first line

The entry program is a module like any other. Reported once per function,
at its first undischarged edge.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * Calling linux, std::linux::sys or a transport from an ordinary program
    or module (for printing, use io or std::linux::io, which mediate it).
  * A helper without a context making the call for a function that has
    one: the context must be on the function that makes the call.
  * A context of another module in the context section: only the
    calling module's own contexts mediate.
  * Calling an `extern c fn` whose contract lets it retain a pointer
    (`captures`, or an unknown escape).

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
`botlish -direct FILE.bot` prints every module's declared and
inferred directness and every call into a direct module, with the
module-owned context that mediates it or "undischarged".

== WHAT TO DO
Either mediate: declare a context struct in this module and require it in
the function that makes the call (`fn emit(text: str, context io:
LocalIO)`), installing it at the top level; or declare the whole file
`direct module` when it is deliberately low-level. There is no per-call or
per-function exemption.

@example reject
import std::abi
import std::abi::bytes
import std::linux
import std::str

fn emit(text: str) -> int:
    std::linux::write(std::abi::i32(1), std::abi::bytes::from_list(std::str::encode_utf8(text)))

0
@caption std::linux::write called without a context of the entry program
@error CORE SEMANTIC DIRECT
@at 7:5
@end

@topic error.direct-callable-value
title: DIRECT-CALLABLE-VALUE: a direct module's callable used as a value
summary: A module that is not declared direct may call a direct module's function but may not pass, return, store or capture it as a value, which would carry the authority past its context boundary unseen.
index: errors
alias: is a callable of the direct module
keywords: direct module, function value, pass, return, store, capture, authority
related: concept.direct-modules error.direct error.context-function-value
docs: DIRECT-MODULES.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC DIRECT-CALLABLE-VALUE

== MEANING
Direct edges are exact calls. A function value of a direct module could be
called anywhere, through code the analysis cannot see:

    std::linux::write is a callable of the direct module "std::linux": the entry
    program, which is not declared direct, may only call it (or bind it to
    another name as a statement), never pass, return, store or capture it
    as a value -- a call through an unknown function value would carry
    direct authority past this module's context boundary unseen

== WHAT TO DO
Call the function directly, in a function that requires one of this
module's contexts; pass data, not the callable. A module declared `direct
module` may take a direct callable as a value.

@example reject
import std::abi
import std::abi::bytes
import std::linux

fn pick():
    std::linux::write

0
@caption std::linux::write returned as a value
@error CORE SEMANTIC DIRECT-CALLABLE-VALUE
@at 6:5
@end

@topic error.stale-direct-module
title: STALE-DIRECT-MODULE: a direct module declaration nothing needs
summary: `direct module` must state exactly what the compiler infers: a file that makes no unmediated call into a direct module must not declare it.
index: errors
alias: direct module is unnecessary
keywords: direct module, stale, marker, declaration, inference, mediated
related: concept.direct-modules error.direct error.direct-module-position
docs: DIRECT-MODULES.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC STALE-DIRECT-MODULE

== MEANING
The marker is an exact declaration, not a permission bit: a module never
silently becomes direct when a call is added (DIRECT), and a marker never
outlives the authority it acknowledged:

    `direct module` is unnecessary: the entry program makes no call into a
    direct module, so it exposes no direct authority; remove the
    declaration (it must state exactly what the compiler infers)

When the module does call direct modules but every call is mediated by its
own contexts, the message says that instead. This is a whole-file
property, reported at the marker; a `static compile:` block can never
produce it.

== WHAT TO DO
Remove the `direct module` line (`-direct FILE.bot` shows what the
compiler infers). If the module is meant to be low-level, the marker
returns with its first unmediated direct call.

@example reject
direct module

0
@caption A program with no direct call declared direct
@error CORE SEMANTIC STALE-DIRECT-MODULE
@at 1:1
@end

@topic error.direct-module-form
title: DIRECT-MODULE-FORM: direct module followed by something
summary: `direct module` is exactly two words on a line of its own: a module's identity is its file path, so the declaration takes no name and nothing follows it.
index: errors
alias: direct module takes no name
keywords: direct module, syntax, module name, file path
related: concept.direct-modules error.direct-module-position error.duplicate-direct-module error.surface-syntax
docs: DIRECT-MODULES.md
code: DIRECT-MODULE-FORM
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | DIRECT-MODULE-FORM: *

== MEANING
There is no `module NAME` declaration in Botlish: stdlib/linux.bot is the
module std::linux because of where it is. So:

    `direct module` takes no name: a module's identity is its file path
    (stdlib/linux.bot is the module std::linux), so the declaration is the
    two words alone
    expected end of line after `direct module`, found ...

== WHAT TO DO
Write the two words alone on the file's first line.

@example reject
direct module linux
0
@caption A name after direct module
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 1:15
@end

@topic error.direct-module-position
title: DIRECT-MODULE-POSITION: direct module not at the start of the file
summary: `direct module` declares the whole file's module direct, so it is written once, as the file's first declaration (only comments and blank lines before it), never inside a function or block.
index: errors
alias: direct module must be the file's first declaration
keywords: direct module, position, first line, import, function, block
related: concept.direct-modules error.direct-module-form error.duplicate-direct-module error.direct error.surface-syntax
docs: DIRECT-MODULES.md
code: DIRECT-MODULE-POSITION
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | DIRECT-MODULE-POSITION: *

== MEANING
Directness is a property of the module, visible to a reviewer at the top
of the file. The parser rejects the marker anywhere else:

    `direct module` must be the file's first declaration, before its
    imports and every other declaration or statement (only comments and
    blank lines may precede it), and is written at most once

Inside a function, loop, branch, handler or static block the message adds
that there is no function-, block- or call-level form.

== WHAT TO DO
Move the line above the imports. There is no narrower form: mediate the
calls through a context of the module instead (DIRECT).

@example reject
import std::list
direct module
0
@caption direct module after an import
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 2:1
@end

