@topic error.extern-syntax
title: EXTERN-SYNTAX: a malformed extern c fn or extern c library declaration
summary: `extern c fn NAME [named "SYMBOL"](PARAMS) -> TYPE [FACT]` and `extern c library "NAME"` are the only extern forms; anything else is a located syntax error naming the remedy.
index: errors
alias: extern declaration names its foreign ABI; the only foreign ABI is c
keywords: extern, c, fn, library, named, symbol, linker, noescape, captures, read, write, syntax, FFI
related: concept.c-ffi error.foreign-borrow error.surface-syntax backend.native-only
docs: C-FFI.md C-ABI-TYPES.md
code: EXTERN-SYNTAX
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | EXTERN-SYNTAX: *

== MEANING
An extern declaration is a truthful C ABI declaration with no body:

    extern c library "crypto"
    extern c fn sum named "bcf_sum"(data: std::ffi::c::Ptr[std::ffi::c::UChar]
        read noescape, count: std::ffi::c::ULong) -> std::ffi::c::ULong

The parser reports each malformed form at the offender, for example:

    an extern declaration names its foreign ABI: write "extern c fn
    NAME(...) -> T" (the only foreign ABI is c)
    expected the library's name as a string after "extern c library" ...
    an extern c fn is a declaration with no body: it has no trailing ":"
    duplicate "named": an extern c fn has one link symbol

Also reported: a library given as a path or a linker option, a symbol that
is not a valid link symbol, `fn` or `library` missing after `extern c`, a
fact stated twice, and a malformed variadic marker -- C's `...` (written
`variadic` here), `variadic` with no fixed parameter before it, anything
after it, or a name or type on it (C's trailing arguments have none; each
call passes its own C types, concept.variadics).

== WHAT TO DO
Follow the grammar in concept.c-ffi: `c` is the only ABI, the library is
the linker's name as a string (`"crypto"` for -lcrypto), every parameter
and the result are typed (`-> unit` for void), and there is no body and no
`errors` clause. A program that calls C runs on the native backends only;
a declaration that is never called compiles anywhere.

@example reject
extern fn f() -> unit
0
@caption The ABI is missing
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 1:8
@end

@topic error.foreign-borrow
title: FOREIGN-BORROW: bytes passed to a C pointer that does not admit them
summary: Bytes or MutableBytes reach a C pointer parameter for one call only if its contract says noescape, a known access (read or write) and a byte-sized pointee; this argument fails one.
index: errors
alias: byte storage is exposed to C only; temporary byte exposure refused
keywords: FFI, extern c fn, std::abi::bytes::Bytes, MutableBytes, noescape, captures, access, read, write, pointer, borrow, exposure, alias, independent
related: concept.c-ffi error.extern-syntax error.direct error.imprint stdlib.module.std::ffi::c stdlib.module.std::abi::bytes
docs: C-POINTER-BORROWING.md C-FFI.md C-ABI-TYPES.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC FOREIGN-BORROW

== MEANING
A foreign call may borrow an existing std::abi::bytes::Bytes or MutableBytes
for the duration of the call -- no pointer value of Botlish storage is
ever made, and no pointer may outlive the call. The contract of the
parameter decides, and nothing else (not a `const`, a name or a C type):

    escape      noescape or proven noescape; `captures` or unknown is
                refused (unknown is never "probably synchronous")
    access      read or write; unknown is refused
    write       needs a MutableBytes: C writes a private copy, which the
                call returns (`written.out`); an immutable Bytes is read
                only
    pointee     std::ffi::c::Char, SChar, UChar or Void

A pointer result of a call that exposes storage must say where it comes
from (`independent`, or `alias PARAM`). The refusal names the argument and
the reason, e.g.:

    argument 1 of fill: parameter "p" lets C modify the bytes (access
    write), and an immutable std::abi::bytes::Bytes is exposed only for
    reading: expose a std::abi::bytes::MutableBytes, whose written bytes the
    call returns

This is a compile-time check: it is reported on every backend, although
only the native backends can run a C call.

== WHAT TO DO
Pass the owner the contract admits (a MutableBytes for write access); if
the contract is wrong, fix the declaration's facts -- they are your
promise about the C function, so state what its documentation guarantees.
A parameter C may keep cannot take Botlish storage at all.

@example reject
import std::abi
import std::abi::bytes
import std::ffi::c

extern c fn fill(
    p: std::ffi::c::Ptr[std::ffi::c::UChar] write noescape,
    n: std::ffi::c::ULong
) -> unit

fn give(data: std::abi::bytes::Bytes):
    fill(data, 4)

0
@caption C writes through p; an immutable Bytes is exposed for reading only
@error CORE SEMANTIC FOREIGN-BORROW
@at 11:10
@end

@topic error.imprint
title: IMPRINT: a call's imprint variables cannot be bound
summary: A signature with imprint variables (T in `List[T]`) is imprinted at each call: the arguments must establish every variable consistently, or the call supplies them explicitly as `f[TYPE](...)`.
index: errors
alias: imprint variable is not established; imprinted callable is not a value; explicit imprints
keywords: imprint, imprint variable, T, generic, List[T], std::ffi::c::null, std::ffi::c::Ptr, explicit, f[int]
related: concept.c-ffi concept.specialization concept.function-types error.type error.foreign-borrow stdlib.module.std::ffi::c
docs: C-ABI-TYPES.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC IMPRINT

== MEANING
An imprint variable is a one-letter capital name (T, K, T2) in a
signature, naming no visible type: whatever type a particular call
imprints there. It is not a declared generic parameter -- Botlish has no
`fn f[T]` -- and the body is checked once, for every T. A variable is
established by an argument whose parameter holds it in an applied type
(`List[T]`, `MutableArray[T]`, `std::ffi::c::Ptr[T]`, a parameterized
struct's application `Map[K, V]`, ...), left to right; a variable that
occurs only in the result is supplied at the call. An empty List says
nothing about its element. IMPRINT reports a call that cannot bind them,
a parameterized struct's construction whose field values do not establish
a slot (concept.parameterized-structs), or a callable used as a value:

    empty: imprint variable T is not established (it occurs only in the
    result, and no type named T is visible where empty is declared): write
    empty[T](...) with explicit imprints
    both: imprint variable T is int by an earlier argument but str by
    argument 2
    empty takes 1 imprint(s), [T] (in the order they first occur in its
    signature), but 2 are given
    plus has no imprint variables, so it takes no imprint plus[...]
    first has imprint variables, so it is not a value: an imprinted
    callable is only called, where each call imprints its variables (wrap
    the call in a function to pass it on)

A parameter declared with a bare variable (`fn identity(x: T) -> T`)
establishes nothing; that declaration is a TYPE error, not IMPRINT. A
binding that makes an application of the signature invalid (K bound to a
function where a slot requires Hashable) is TRAIT-NOT-SATISFIED, not
IMPRINT: the variables were established.

A nested function's signature names its enclosing function's variable as
that variable: rigid there, never bound at a call of the nested function,
every argument held to it (a TYPE error otherwise). Only the variables the
enclosing functions do not have are the nested function's own; one with no
variable of its own takes no imprint (`g[int]()` is IMPRINT).

== WHAT TO DO
Give explicit imprints, all of them, in first-occurrence order:
`empty[int]()`, `std::ffi::c::null[std::ffi::c::Void]()`. Make the arguments
agree. To pass an imprinted function on, wrap one call of it in an ordinary
function.

@example reject
import std::list

fn empty() -> List[T]:
    []

xs = empty()
xs
@caption T occurs only in the result
@error CORE SEMANTIC IMPRINT
@at 6:6
@end

@example accept
import std::list

fn empty() -> List[T]:
    []

xs = empty[int]()
xs
@caption The imprint supplied at the call
@value []
@end

@topic error.layout-size
title: LAYOUT-SIZE: a layout's size is not a valid byte count
summary: A `layout` declares its size explicitly with `size N`, a byte count from 1 to 2147483647; zero or a larger number is rejected at the size.
index: errors
alias: layout has size
keywords: layout, size, binary, bytes, declaration
related: concept.binary-layouts error.layout-alignment error.layout-field-bounds stdlib.module.std::binary
docs: BINARY-LAYOUT.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-SIZE

== MEANING
A layout describes a fixed number of physical bytes, stated, never
computed from an ABI:

    layout "Header" has size 0: a layout's size is a byte count from 1 to
    2147483647

A layout error is a declaration error: it rejects the whole program and is
never a `static compile:` block's rejection (a layout is top level only).

== WHAT TO DO
State the real size of the record in bytes; every field must fit inside it
(LAYOUT-FIELD-BOUNDS).

@example reject
import std::binary

layout Header:
    size 0
    align 4
    field magic: std::binary::U32 at 0

0
@caption A layout of zero bytes
@error CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-SIZE
@at 4:10
@end

@topic error.layout-alignment
title: LAYOUT-ALIGNMENT: a layout's alignment is not a power of two
summary: `align N` must be a power of two from 1 to 268435456.
index: errors
alias: layout has alignment
keywords: layout, align, alignment, power of two, binary
related: concept.binary-layouts error.layout-size stdlib.module.std::binary
docs: BINARY-LAYOUT.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-ALIGNMENT

== MEANING
    layout "Header" has alignment 3: an alignment is a power of two from 1
    to 268435456

The alignment is recorded and reported (std::binary::layout_alignment);
there is deliberately no rule that the size be a multiple of it or that
field offsets be aligned, so packed and vendor layouts are legal as stated.

== WHAT TO DO
Use the record's real alignment: 1, 2, 4, 8, ...

@example reject
import std::binary

layout Header:
    size 8
    align 3
    field magic: std::binary::U32 at 0

0
@caption 3 is not a power of two
@error CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-ALIGNMENT
@at 5:11
@end

@topic error.layout-field-type
title: LAYOUT-FIELD-TYPE: a layout field's type has no fixed-width encoding
summary: A layout field is a scalar whose integer domain is exactly one fixed-width encoding's value set (std::binary::U8 .. U64, I8 .. I64, or an equal `Int in a..b`), a nested layout, or a C pointer.
index: errors
alias: cannot have type; no fixed width
keywords: layout, field, int, std::binary::U32, domain, encoding, width, signed, unsigned, nested layout
related: concept.binary-layouts concept.integer-domains error.layout-cycle stdlib.module.std::binary
docs: BINARY-LAYOUT.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-FIELD-TYPE

== MEANING
The encoding of a field follows from its type's interval alone:
`0..2^(8k)-1` is unsigned and `-2^(8k-1)..2^(8k-1)-1` two's complement, k
in 1, 2, 4, 8. Because the domain is exactly the encoding's value set,
every bit pattern parses and every value emits, with no check. Anything
else is rejected at the type:

    field "magic" of layout "Header" cannot have type int: int has no
    fixed width: a scalar field is an integer domain whose values are
    exactly one fixed-width encoding's (e.g. std::binary::U32, Int in
    0..4294967295)

Also rejected: a narrower or exact-set domain (`Int in 0..100`), a
refinement, str, bool, a struct, `LayoutStruct[L]` (write the layout L
itself) and an unknown name. A C pointer field (std::ffi::c::Ptr[T],
std::ffi::c::FnPtr[F]) is accepted, and makes the layout native-only
(error.layout-native-only).

== WHAT TO DO
Use std::binary::U8 .. std::binary::U64 or std::binary::I8 ..
std::binary::I64 (or your own domain with exactly that interval). Check a
narrower range after parsing, in ordinary code.

@example reject
import std::binary

layout Header:
    size 8
    align 4
    field magic: int at 0

0
@caption int is unbounded
@error CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-FIELD-TYPE
@at 6:18
@end

@topic error.layout-field-bounds
title: LAYOUT-FIELD-BOUNDS: a layout field lies outside the layout
summary: Every field's bytes (its offset plus its width) must lie inside the layout's declared size, and an offset is a byte count from 0.
index: errors
alias: outside the layout's size
keywords: layout, field, offset, width, size, bounds
related: concept.binary-layouts error.layout-size error.layout-field-overlap stdlib.module.std::binary
docs: BINARY-LAYOUT.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-FIELD-BOUNDS

== MEANING
    field "magic" of layout "Header" occupies bytes 6..9 (offset 6, width
    4), outside the layout's size 8

The message names the bytes the field would occupy, so the fix is
visible: move the field or enlarge the layout.

@example reject
import std::binary

layout Header:
    size 8
    align 4
    field magic: std::binary::U32 at 6

0
@caption Four bytes at offset 6 end past byte 7
@error CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-FIELD-BOUNDS
@at 6:38
@end

@topic error.layout-field-overlap
title: LAYOUT-FIELD-OVERLAP: two layout fields share a byte
summary: The fields of a layout never overlap: layouts have no union or overlay, so two fields covering a common byte are rejected at the later one.
index: errors
alias: overlaps field
keywords: layout, field, overlap, union, overlay, offset
related: concept.binary-layouts error.layout-field-bounds stdlib.module.std::binary
docs: BINARY-LAYOUT.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-FIELD-OVERLAP

== MEANING
    field "count" of layout "Header" (bytes 2..3) overlaps field "magic"
    (bytes 0..3): the fields of a layout never share a byte (an overlay
    would be an explicit union construct, which layouts do not have)

== WHAT TO DO
Fix the offsets. To read the same bytes two ways, declare two layouts and
parse the data with each.

@example reject
import std::binary

layout Header:
    size 8
    align 4
    field magic: std::binary::U32 at 0
    field count: std::binary::U16 at 2

0
@caption count's bytes are inside magic's
@error CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-FIELD-OVERLAP
@at 7:38
@end

@topic error.layout-cycle
title: LAYOUT-CYCLE: a layout contains itself by value
summary: A nested layout field holds the inner layout's bytes by value, so a layout that contains itself, directly or through others, would have no finite size.
index: errors
alias: contains itself by value
keywords: layout, nested, recursive, cycle, pointer
related: concept.binary-layouts error.layout-field-type stdlib.module.std::binary
docs: BINARY-LAYOUT.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-CYCLE

== MEANING
    layout "Node" contains itself by value through Node -> Node: a fixed
    layout has a finite size, so it can never contain itself (a reference
    to one is a pointer field, not a nested layout)

The chain names every layout on the cycle. A link to another record is a
pointer field (`field next: std::ffi::c::Ptr[Node] at 0`), which makes the
layout native-only: it is read through a native view
(error.layout-native-only).

@example reject
layout Node:
    size 8
    align 4
    field next: Node at 0

0
@caption Node holds a Node by value
@error CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-CYCLE
@at 4:17
@end

@topic error.layout-array-extent
title: LAYOUT-ARRAY-EXTENT: a fixed array's extent is not a positive size
summary: A fixed array's extent is a compile-time integer of at least 1 whose width fits a layout; a zero-length or flexible array is not a fixed array.
index: errors
alias: a fixed array's extent is a positive integer
keywords: layout, array, extent, zero-length, flexible array member, length, fixed array
related: concept.binary-layouts error.layout-field-bounds error.layout-array-index stdlib.module.std::binary
docs: FIXED-LAYOUT-ARRAYS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-ARRAY-EXTENT

== MEANING
    field "items" of layout "Packet" is an array of 0 elements: a fixed
    array's extent is a positive integer (a C zero-length or flexible
    array is not a fixed array)

The extent is a literal of any size, multiplied exactly (never wrapped):
an array wider than the largest layout is rejected here too. An array
whose elements are arrays says so ("the elements of field ... are arrays
of 0 elements"). The same rule holds for the live array type
NativeView[array N of E].

== WHAT TO DO
Give the array its real extent. A C flexible array member or a
variable-length tail is not a fixed layout: describe the fixed header as a
layout and read the tail separately (its length is data, not
representation).

@example reject
import std::binary

layout Packet:
    size 8
    align 1
    field items: array 0 of std::binary::U8 at 0

0
@caption An array of no elements
@error CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-ARRAY-EXTENT
@at 6:18
@end

@example accept
import std::binary

layout Packet:
    size 8
    align 1
    field kind: std::binary::U8 at 0
    field items: array 7 of std::binary::U8 at 1

std::binary::layout_length(Packet)
@value 8
@caption Seven one-byte elements after the kind byte
@end

@topic error.layout-array-index
title: LAYOUT-ARRAY-INDEX: a static element index outside its fixed array
summary: An element designator (L.items[3], L.m[1][2]) names its index statically, so an index outside 0..N-1 is rejected where it is written.
index: errors
alias: the element index lies outside the fixed array
keywords: layout, array, index, designator, out of range, element, subscript
related: concept.binary-layouts error.layout-array-extent stdlib.std::binary::store stdlib.std::binary::write stdlib.std::binary::element
docs: FIXED-LAYOUT-ARRAYS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-ARRAY-INDEX

== MEANING
    Packet.items is a fixed array of 4 elements (indices 0..3): the element
    index [4] lies outside it

The designator of std::binary::write, std::binary::write_foreign and
std::binary::store is static: its indices are part of the field it names,
resolved to one offset at compile time. A dynamic index is
std::binary::element or std::binary::store_element on a live array, checked
at run time (IndexNotFound) unless the compiler proves it.

@example reject
import std::abi
import std::abi::bytes
import std::binary

layout Packet:
    size 4
    align 1
    field items: array 4 of std::binary::U8 at 0

fn last(d: std::abi::bytes::MutableBytes) -> std::abi::bytes::MutableBytes errors LayoutLengthMismatch:
    std::binary::write(d, Packet.items[4], 1)

0
@caption Four elements: the last is items[3]
@error CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-ARRAY-INDEX
@at 11:40
@end

@topic error.layout-native-only
title: LAYOUT-NATIVE-ONLY: bytes and a C pointer field, the ordinary way
summary: A pointer field is never parsed from bytes (that would forge a pointer) nor emitted or written into them as ordinary data: std::binary::write_foreign is the explicit encoding.
index: errors
alias: cannot imprint layout; is a C pointer
keywords: layout, pointer, std::ffi::c::Ptr, FnPtr, parse, emit, native view, forge
related: concept.binary-layouts stdlib.std::binary::native::view stdlib.std::binary::load error.layout-field-type
docs: NATIVE-LAYOUT-VIEWS.md BINARY-LAYOUT.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-NATIVE-ONLY

== MEANING
    std::binary::parse cannot imprint layout "Link" from bytes: its field "next"
    is a C pointer, and imprinting arbitrary bytes into it would make a
    pointer no foreign code handed out; a layout with a pointer field is
    read through a native view of live native memory (std::binary::native::view,
    std::binary::load)

Any bit pattern can arrive in byte storage, so a pointer read from bytes
would be forged. A pointer field nested inside another layout makes the
outer layout native-only too; the message names the field's path. Emitting
such a layout is rejected the same way, and so is an ordinary
std::binary::write of the pointer field itself (NATIVE-LAYOUT-WRITES.md): an
existing pointer is encoded into bytes only by std::binary::write_foreign.

== WHAT TO DO
Read the record where foreign code put it: std::binary::native::view(L, p)
of the pointer that code handed out, then its fields or std::binary::load.
To parse bytes, declare a second layout whose pointer field is an Int
(std::binary::U64 at the same offset): its value is inert address bits,
never a pointer.

@example reject
import std::abi::bytes
import std::binary
import std::ffi::c

layout Link:
    size 8
    align 8
    field next: std::ffi::c::Ptr[std::ffi::c::Void] at 0

fn first(d: std::abi::bytes::Bytes) -> bool errors LayoutLengthMismatch:
    l = std::binary::parse(Link, d)
    l.next.null?()

0
@caption Bytes would become a pointer
@error CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-NATIVE-ONLY
@at 11:28
@end

@topic error.layout-foreign-write
title: LAYOUT-FOREIGN-WRITE: a foreign write of a field that is not a C pointer
summary: std::binary::write_foreign encodes an existing std::ffi::c::Ptr or FnPtr into a C pointer field of byte storage; an ordinary field is written by std::binary::write.
index: errors
alias: encodes an existing foreign value; is an ordinary field
keywords: write_foreign, pointer field, std::binary::write, encode pointer, foreign write
related: stdlib.std::binary::write_foreign stdlib.std::binary::write error.layout-native-only concept.binary-layouts
docs: NATIVE-LAYOUT-WRITES.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-FOREIGN-WRITE

== MEANING
    std::binary::write_foreign encodes an existing foreign value (a std::ffi::c::Ptr
    or std::ffi::c::FnPtr) into a C pointer field; Pair.count is an ordinary
    field of type int[std::binary::U32], written by std::binary::write

The foreign write is deliberately separate from ordinary serialization:
it is the one operation that puts a pointer's address bits into bytes,
and it takes only a pointer the program already has. Its field must be
one of the layout's C pointer fields.

== WHAT TO DO
Write an ordinary field with std::binary::write(data, L.field, value). Use
std::binary::write_foreign only for a field declared std::ffi::c::Ptr[T] or
std::ffi::c::FnPtr[F], with a pointer of exactly that type.

@example reject
import std::abi::bytes
import std::binary
import std::ffi::c

layout Pair:
    size 16
    align 8
    field count: std::binary::U32 at 0
    field next: std::ffi::c::Ptr[std::ffi::c::Void] at 8

fn f(d: std::abi::bytes::MutableBytes) -> std::abi::bytes::MutableBytes errors LayoutLengthMismatch:
    std::binary::write_foreign(d, Pair.count, 7)

0
@caption count is no pointer field
@error CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-FOREIGN-WRITE
@at 12:35
@end

@topic error.layout-union
title: LAYOUT-UNION: a copy of a union, or of anything holding one
summary: A union is several interpretations of the same bytes, so it has no copied value: no LayoutStruct, parse, emit or load of a union or of a layout holding one; read or write one alternative.
index: errors
alias: has no copied value; is a union layout; holds the union layout
keywords: union, union layout, alternative, overlap, LayoutStruct, std::binary::parse, std::binary::load, std::binary::read, copy, OVERLAPPED
related: concept.layout-unions stdlib.std::binary::read stdlib.std::binary::write concept.binary-layouts error.layout-native-only
docs: LAYOUT-UNIONS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-UNION

== MEANING
    std::binary::parse cannot imprint layout OVL from bytes: layout OVL holds
    the union layout OVLU by value at OVL.u: a union is several
    interpretations of the same bytes, never independent values, so it
    has no copied value; ...

A union's alternatives all start at its first byte: a struct holding one
value per alternative would pretend they are independent fields, and nothing
records which one was written last (a union has no tag and no active
alternative). So a union has no LayoutStruct, and neither has any layout
holding one by value -- directly, in a nested layout or as an array element.
The same holds for std::binary::load of a live union or of an array of
unions, std::binary::read of a designator that holds one, and a whole- array
write of an array of unions.

== WHAT TO DO
Read and write a union one alternative at a time, through a static
designator: std::binary::read(data, OVL.u.offsets.offset) and
std::binary::write(data, OVL.u.offsets.offset, v) on bytes; in native
memory, v.u.offsets.offset through a view and std::binary::store(e,
OVL.u.offsets. offset, v) through an edit. A record alternative or an array
alternative is copied whole (std::binary::read(data, OVL.u.offsets),
std::binary::load(v.u. offsets)): it is an ordinary layout. A higher-level
wrapper that knows which alternative a protocol means turns it into a domain
value.

@example reject
import std::abi::bytes
import std::binary

layout HALVES:
    size 4
    align 2
    field low: std::binary::U16 at 0
    field high: std::binary::U16 at 2

union layout WORD:
    size 4
    align 4
    alternative whole: std::binary::U32
    alternative halves: HALVES

fn f(d: std::abi::bytes::Bytes) -> any errors LayoutLengthMismatch:
    std::binary::parse(WORD, d)

0
@caption A union has no LayoutStruct to imprint into
@error CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-UNION
@at 17:24
@end

@example accept
import std::abi
import std::abi::bytes
import std::binary

layout HALVES:
    size 4
    align 2
    field low: std::binary::U16 at 0
    field high: std::binary::U16 at 2

union layout WORD:
    size 4
    align 4
    alternative whole: std::binary::U32
    alternative halves: HALVES

fn f(d: std::abi::bytes::MutableBytes) -> list errors LayoutLengthMismatch:
    e = std::abi::bytes::freeze(std::binary::write(d, WORD.whole, 131073))
    [std::binary::read(e, WORD.whole), std::binary::read(e, WORD.halves)]

r = f(std::abi::bytes::zeroed(std::abi::usize(4))):
    on LayoutLengthMismatch:
        []
r
@caption One alternative at a time
