@topic error.layout-not-a-type
title: LAYOUT-NOT-A-TYPE: a layout's name used as a type
summary: A layout describes bytes and is not itself a type: the values imprinted with layout L have the type `LayoutStruct[L]`.
index: errors
alias: is a layout, not a type
keywords: layout, LayoutStruct, type annotation, parameter, std::binary::parse
related: concept.binary-layouts error.layout-not-a-value error.layout-struct-parameter stdlib.std::binary::parse
docs: BINARY-LAYOUT.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-NOT-A-TYPE

== MEANING
    unknown or invalid type Header for parameter "h": "Header" is a
    layout, not a type: a layout describes bytes, and the values imprinted
    with it have the type LayoutStruct[Header]

== WHAT TO DO
Annotate with `LayoutStruct[Header]`.

@example reject
import std::binary

layout Header:
    size 8
    align 4
    field magic: std::binary::U32 at 0

fn f(h: Header) -> int:
    0

0
@caption Header as a parameter type
@error CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-NOT-A-TYPE
@at 8:5
@end

@topic error.layout-not-a-value
title: LAYOUT-NOT-A-VALUE: a layout's name used as a value
summary: No runtime layout object exists: a layout is named only in a type (`LayoutStruct[L]`) or as the static layout operand of a binary operation.
index: errors
alias: is a layout, not a value
keywords: layout, value, std::binary::parse, layout_length, method syntax, Header.parse
related: concept.binary-layouts error.layout-not-a-type error.layout-operand stdlib.std::binary::parse
docs: BINARY-LAYOUT.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-NOT-A-VALUE

== MEANING
    "Header" is a layout, not a value: a layout is named in a type
    (LayoutStruct[Header]) or as the layout operand of a binary operation
    (std::binary::parse(Header, data), std::binary::layout_length(Header)), and no
    runtime layout object exists

`x = Header`, `[Header]`, `Header(...)` and `Header.parse(d)` (method
syntax would make the layout a receiver value) are all this error.

== WHAT TO DO
Write the layout at the operation: `std::binary::parse(Header, data)`. To
vary the layout, write one call per layout.

@example reject
import std::binary

layout Header:
    size 8
    align 4
    field magic: std::binary::U32 at 0

x = Header
0
@caption A layout bound to a name
@error CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-NOT-A-VALUE
@at 8:5
@end

@topic error.layout-operand
title: LAYOUT-OPERAND: a binary operation's layout operand is not a layout name
summary: The layout argument of std::binary::parse, std::binary::layout_length and std::binary::layout_alignment is static evidence written at the call: a layout's name, never a variable, string or expression.
index: errors
alias: layout operand must be the name of a layout
keywords: layout, std::binary::parse, operand, static, parameter, value
related: concept.binary-layouts error.layout-not-a-value error.unknown-layout stdlib.std::binary::parse
docs: BINARY-LAYOUT.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-OPERAND

== MEANING
    the layout operand of std::binary::parse must be the name of a layout, but
    "which" is a value here: the layout is static evidence, never a
    computed value

Every compiled backend reads the layout's descriptor at compile time, so
the operand cannot be chosen at run time.

@example reject
import std::abi::bytes
import std::binary

layout Header:
    size 8
    align 4
    field magic: std::binary::U32 at 0

fn f(data: std::abi::bytes::Bytes, which):
    std::binary::parse(which, data)

0
@caption A parameter as the layout
@error CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-OPERAND
@at 10:24
@end

@topic error.layout-struct-parameter
title: LAYOUT-STRUCT-PARAMETER: LayoutStruct applied to something other than a layout
summary: `LayoutStruct[L]` is a built-in applied type whose one argument must name a `layout` declaration; a type, struct, enum or refinement is rejected at the annotation.
index: errors
alias: the argument of LayoutStruct must name a layout declaration
keywords: LayoutStruct, layout, type argument, struct, generic
related: concept.binary-layouts error.unknown-layout error.layout-not-a-type
docs: BINARY-LAYOUT.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-STRUCT-PARAMETER

== MEANING
LayoutStruct is not a generic struct facility: its argument is a layout,
not a type parameter, and the message says what the argument is instead:

    unknown or invalid type LayoutStruct[int] for parameter "v":
    LayoutStruct[int]: int is a built-in type, not a layout; the argument
    of LayoutStruct must name a layout declaration (`layout Name:`), whose
    fields LayoutStruct[Name] has

An unknown name is UNKNOWN-LAYOUT instead.

@example reject
import std::binary

fn f(v: LayoutStruct[int]) -> int:
    0

0
@caption int is not a layout
@error CORE SEMANTIC LAYOUT-STRUCT-PARAMETER
@at 3:5
@end

@topic error.unknown-layout
title: UNKNOWN-LAYOUT: a layout name that denotes no visible layout
summary: A layout operand of a binary operation, or the argument of `LayoutStruct[...]`, names no visible `layout` declaration.
index: errors
alias: unknown layout
keywords: layout, std::binary::parse, LayoutStruct, qualified, import, module
related: concept.binary-layouts error.layout-operand error.layout-struct-parameter concept.imports
docs: BINARY-LAYOUT.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC UNKNOWN-LAYOUT

== MEANING
    unknown layout "Missing" (the layout operand of std::binary::parse): no
    "layout Missing:" declaration is visible
    unknown layout "Missing" in LayoutStruct[Missing]: ...

A module's layout is named qualified once the module is imported
(`std::binary::parse(geo::Box, data)`); `import type` does not reach
layouts.

== WHAT TO DO
Fix the spelling, declare the layout, or import its module and qualify it.

@example reject
import std::abi::bytes
import std::binary

fn f(data: std::abi::bytes::Bytes):
    std::binary::parse(Missing, data)

0
@caption No layout named Missing
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNKNOWN-LAYOUT
@at 5:24
@end

@topic error.lockstep-unequal
title: LOCKSTEP-UNEQUAL: lockstep clauses provably iterate different numbers of times
summary: A lockstep loop (`loop a in xs and i from 0 to n:`) needs every clause to have the same iteration count; the compiler proved two counts differ.
index: errors
alias: clause 1 iterates
keywords: lockstep, and, loop, iteration count, cardinality, zip, enumerate, length
related: concept.lockstep proof.lockstep error.lockstep-unproven concept.loops task.iterate-with-index
docs: COLLECTING-LOOPS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC LOCKSTEP-UNEQUAL

== MEANING
`and` advances every clause together, once per body execution. There is
no shortest-wins rule, no padding and no run-time length check: a lockstep
loop is legal exactly when the compiler proves the counts equal. When it
proves them different (two constants, or forms that differ by a constant):

    lockstep loop requires equal iteration counts; clause 1 iterates 3
    times but clause 2 iterates 2 times

== WHAT TO DO
Make the ranges agree, typically `from 0 to xs.length()` beside
`x in xs`. For a pairwise walk of lists of different lengths, iterate the
shorter count with an index and read both lists (handling IndexNotFound
where the proof does not reach).

@example reject
loop x in [1, 2, 3] and i from 0 to 2:
    x + i
@caption Three elements against two indices
@error CORE SEMANTIC LOCKSTEP-UNEQUAL
@at 1:1
@end

@example accept
import std::list

fn indexed(xs: List[int]) -> List[int]:
    loop x in xs and i from 0 to xs.length():
        x * i

indexed([4, 5, 6])
@caption An element and its index
@value [0, 5, 12]
@end

@topic error.lockstep-unproven
title: LOCKSTEP-UNPROVEN: lockstep clauses not proven to iterate equally often
summary: The compiler could not prove the clauses of a lockstep loop have equal iteration counts; equal lengths at run time do not matter, only what is proven.
index: errors
alias: could not prove; lockstep equal counts
keywords: lockstep, and, loop, iteration count, cardinality, length, parameter, proof, zip
related: concept.lockstep proof.lockstep error.lockstep-unequal concept.loops task.iterate-with-index
docs: COLLECTING-LOOPS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC LOCKSTEP-UNPROVEN

== MEANING
    lockstep loop requires equal iteration counts; could not prove
    length(xs) == length(ys) (clause 1 vs clause 2)

Each clause's count is a linear form over atoms: a List's length
(followed through immutable aliases), an Int binding, constants. Two
counts are equal only when the forms are identical. Not known today:
lengths of two different parameters, a struct field's List, a list::
helper's result, a call (two calls of `f(x)` are two atoms), branch facts
(`if n >= 0:`). A `ys = loop x in xs:` with no break or continue has
exactly xs's length; one that may skip does not.

== WHAT TO DO
Write the clauses over the same List: `loop x in xs and i from 0 to
xs.length():`. For two lists, iterate one and index the other, handling
IndexNotFound (or use std::list::get with a default). There is no run-time
check to opt into.

@example reject
import std::list

fn pairs(xs: List[int], ys: List[int]) -> List[int]:
    loop x in xs and y in ys:
        x + y

pairs([1], [2])
@caption Two parameters' lengths are unrelated
@error CORE SEMANTIC LOCKSTEP-UNPROVEN
@at 4:5
@end

@topic error.malformed-flag
title: MALFORMED-FLAG: a flag spelled without its name glued to the colon
summary: A flag is written `:name`, the name directly after the colon: a space after the colon, a number or a keyword as the name is a syntax error at the colon.
index: errors
alias: flag is written :name; no space after the colon
keywords: flag, colon, :name, syntax, keyword, space
related: concept.flags rule.flags error.malformed-flag-section error.flag-not-a-value error.surface-syntax
docs: FLAGS.md
code: MALFORMED-FLAG
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | MALFORMED-FLAG: *

== MEANING
In a flag section (`fn f(x, flags :quiet)`) and in a call (`f(1,
:quiet)`), a flag is one token pair: `:` and a name glued to it.

    a flag is written :a with no space after ":"
    expected a flag name after ":", found integer 5: a flag is written
    :name

== WHAT TO DO
Remove the space, or use a valid name (keywords such as `if` are not
names).

@example reject
fn f(x, flags :quiet):
    x

f(1, : quiet)
@caption A space after the colon
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 4:6
@end

@topic error.malformed-flag-section
title: MALFORMED-FLAG-SECTION: flags declared in the wrong place
summary: A parameter list is ordinary parameters, then one `flags :a, :b` section; a parameter after the flags, a second flags section or a flag outside the section is a syntax error.
index: errors
alias: cannot follow the flags section; flag outside a flag section
keywords: flags, flag section, parameter list, order, syntax
related: concept.flags rule.flags error.malformed-flag error.malformed-context-section error.surface-syntax
docs: FLAGS.md
code: MALFORMED-FLAG-SECTION
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | MALFORMED-FLAG-SECTION: *

== MEANING
    parameter "y" cannot follow the flags section: a parameter list is
    ordinary parameters, then flags
    a "flags" section cannot follow the flags section: a parameter list
    has at most one flags section, after the ordinary parameters
    flag :a is outside a flag section: declare flags after the ordinary
    parameters as "flags :a"

`flags` is contextual: it opens the section only when spelled `flags
:NAME` (a space, then a colon glued to the name). `fn f(flags)` and `fn
f(flags: int)` are ordinary parameters named flags.

== WHAT TO DO
Move ordinary parameters before the section and list every flag in one
section: `fn f(x, y, flags :a, :b)`.

@example reject
fn f(x, flags :a, y):
    x

0
@caption An ordinary parameter after the flags
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 1:19
@end

@topic error.argument-after-flag
title: ARGUMENT-AFTER-FLAG: an ordinary argument after a call flag
summary: Flags come last in a call: `f(x, :a)`, never `f(:a, x)`.
index: errors
alias: ordinary argument cannot follow the flag
keywords: flag, call, argument order, syntax
related: concept.flags rule.flags error.flag-not-a-value error.surface-syntax
docs: FLAGS.md
code: ARGUMENT-AFTER-FLAG
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | ARGUMENT-AFTER-FLAG: *

== MEANING
    an ordinary argument cannot follow the flag :a: flags come last in a
    call

A call mirrors the declaration's order: ordinary arguments, then flags.

@example reject
fn f(x, flags :a):
    x

f(:a, 1)
@caption The flag first
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 4:7
@end

@topic error.flag-not-a-value
title: FLAG-NOT-A-VALUE: a flag written where a value is expected
summary: `:name` exists only as a trailing argument of a call; it is not a value that can be bound, passed in a list or computed with.
index: errors
alias: is a call flag, not a value
keywords: flag, value, symbol, keyword argument, :name
related: concept.flags rule.flags error.argument-after-flag error.surface-syntax
docs: FLAGS.md
code: FLAG-NOT-A-VALUE
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | FLAG-NOT-A-VALUE: *

== MEANING
A flag is a callable-interface category with no runtime representation:
inside the callee it is an immutable Bool local, true when the call wrote
the flag. There is no symbol or FlagSet value:

    :a is a call flag, not a value: flags can only be written as the last
    arguments of a call, e.g. f(x, :a)

== WHAT TO DO
Write the flag in the call. To choose a flag at run time, branch and write
two calls, or use an ordinary Bool or enum parameter instead.

@example reject
fn f(x, flags :a):
    x

y = :a
0
@caption A flag bound to a name
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 4:5
@end

@topic error.static-intrinsic-form
title: STATIC-INTRINSIC-FORM: a malformed static compile block
summary: A static intrinsic is written `static compile:` followed by an indented block on the next lines: no arguments, and no code on the colon's line.
index: errors
alias: static compile takes no arguments; static intrinsic names its operation
keywords: static, static compile, block, indentation, syntax
related: concept.static-compile error.static-unknown-operation concept.negative-tests error.surface-syntax
docs: STATIC-COMPILE.md
code: STATIC-INTRINSIC-FORM
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | STATIC-INTRINSIC-FORM: *

== MEANING
`static compile:` captures the indented block after it; the block is
compiled with the program and replaced by its body (accepted) or by `fail
std::compiler::CompilerError {...}` (rejected). The parser reports:

    "static compile" takes no arguments: it captures the indented block
    written after "static compile:"
    a static intrinsic names its operation after "static": write "static
    compile:" followed by an indented block
    "static compile:" captures an indented block: start it on a new line
    after the ":", found integer 1

== WHAT TO DO
Write

    static compile:
        CODE UNDER TEST

inside a function that declares `errors std::compiler::CompilerError` (after
`import std::compiler`).

@example reject
x = static compile(1):
    1
x
@caption An argument list after static compile
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 1:19
@end

@topic error.static-unknown-operation
title: STATIC-UNKNOWN-OPERATION: an operation after static that does not exist
summary: The only static operation is `static compile:`; any other word after `static` is rejected, because it is compiler syntax and never a name looked up in scope.
index: errors
alias: unknown static operation
keywords: static, static compile, static parse, static assert, compile-time evaluation
related: concept.static-compile error.static-intrinsic-form error.surface-syntax
docs: STATIC-COMPILE.md
code: STATIC-UNKNOWN-OPERATION
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | STATIC-UNKNOWN-OPERATION: *

== MEANING
    unknown static operation "parse": the static operations are "static
    compile:"; an operation after "static" is compiler syntax, never a
    name looked up in scope

There is no `static parse:`, `static assert:`, compile-time evaluation or
user-defined static operation.

== WHAT TO DO
Use `static compile:` to compile code and observe its acceptance or
rejection; compute values at run time.

@example reject
static parse:
    1
@caption parse is not a static operation
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 1:8
@end

@topic error.list-destructuring
title: LIST-DESTRUCTURING: positional List destructuring is not supported
summary: `[a, b] = value` is rejected by design: a List is a sequence of values, and several results with distinct roles are a struct with named fields, destructured as `{value, error} = result`.
index: errors
alias: positional list destructuring; destructure a list
keywords: destructuring, list, tuple, pattern, multiple return values, struct, named fields, unpack
related: rule.no-list-destructuring principle.name-the-parts concept.destructuring concept.structs concept.lists warning.fixed-arity-list-return error.surface-syntax
docs: STRUCT-DESTRUCTURING.md MULTI-VALUE-RESULTS.md
code: LIST-DESTRUCTURING
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | LIST-DESTRUCTURING: *

== MEANING
A bracketed pattern on the left of `=`, at the start of a statement or
nested as a field's pattern (`{inner: [a, b]} = p`), is rejected by the
parser:

    LIST-DESTRUCTURING: positional List destructuring is not supported;
    use a struct value with named fields instead (a List is for a sequence
    of values; have the function return a struct, e.g. `result =
    get_result()` then `{value, error} = result`)

This is a language policy, not an unfinished feature: positions carry no
meaning a reader can check, while field names do (principle.name-the-parts).
Lists remain the type for sequences: element access (std::list::at,
std::list::get) and iteration are unchanged.

== WHAT TO DO
Return a struct (named or anonymous) and destructure it by field names;
or, for genuine sequences, iterate the List or read elements by index.

@example reject
[a, b] = [1, 2]
a
@caption A List pattern
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 1:1
@end

@example accept
struct Split:
    first: int
    second: int

fn split(n: int) -> Split:
    Split {first: n, second: n * 2}

{first, second} = split(3)
first + second
@caption A struct result destructured by field names
@value 9
@end
